{"step_index":11005,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T16:58:27Z","thinking":"The code block addresses the initial state where no response has started. It checks several conditions: if a process is running, a new model turn has begun, or if data is present. The logic then proceeds to determine if a condition for generating a response has been met.\n\n\nIf a response isn't generated within the expected timeframe, an error is flagged, indicating a potential startup failure. Otherwise, the state transitions to 'RESPONSE_IN_PROGRESS,' signaling that generation has begun. The detection of the first token marks a critical milestone, logged for performance analysis.\n\n\nText generation progress is monitored by comparing the current response to the previous. When the text grows, the new content is logged, and the stability timer is reset. If there's no growth within the expected time, a timeout error is flagged, indicating a potential stall. The state checks if the generation process is still active during the timeout.\n\n\nGeneration stability is tracked using a timer, transitioning to a completed state when the response remains unchanged for a configured duration. During active generation, a stagnation timeout is implemented; the process errors out and halts if there's no progress for a specific period, suggesting a stall. The current text state is always provided to external callers.\n\n\nA critical oversight is that the \"cancel generation\" trigger was not fully integrated, causing premature completion declarations. Timeout mechanisms for stagnation and first-token generation were too aggressive, leading to unintended request abortion. The error handling needs refinement to prevent batch crashes upon encountering timeouts. Client-side request timeouts also need review to ensure they align with expected server processing times.\n\n\nThe state of the \"cancel generation\" control is the primary indicator of message completion, specifically whether the streaming process has finished. There are two conditions to check: if the control is active, meaning the message is still being streamed or if it is disabled, which means the message stream has completed. An active \"cancel generation\" control confirms the message is still in progress.\n\n\nThe response validation function needs to be checked after generation, to assess if the output is correct.  A failed response signifies a message failure. Timeout calculations shouldn't be based on fixed time limits. The process completion is indicated by the \"cancel generation\" control's state.  Batch complexity may vary, rendering time-based timeouts unreliable.\n\n\nThe \"cancel generation\" control is the most reliable indicator of generation state. An algorithm will be developed to precisely detect its state within the DOM.  This detector's purpose is to differentiate between active generation, inactive (disabled) and ready to run states.\n\n\nAn algorithm is being developed to reliably identify the \"cancel generation\" state within the interface, using multiple indicators. The plan is to check for stop-related text within various interface elements, including buttons, tooltips, and icons, while also accounting for disabled states. Further, active loading indicators, such as spinners and typing indicators, will be used as supplementary evidence of an active generation process.\n\n\nThe algorithm now actively monitors interface elements for indications of generation in progress. The presence of a \"cancel\" action, spinner, or \"typing\" indicator suggests active text generation. If the \"cancel generation\" button is active, the streaming process is confirmed to be in progress.\n\n\nThe removal of arbitrary timeouts is crucial because the generation process can involve extended thinking followed by token streaming.  The \"cancel generation\" control's state determines completion; text deltas stream as long as the button is active. After generation, a brief stabilization period is introduced, allowing the DOM to settle before extracting the final text and marking the completion state.  A large safety net timeout is incorporated only for catastrophic failures, never to cut off active generation.  The response validation happens after generation to ensure output integrity.\n\n\nThe response validation will check for the presence of the expected keys and data structures after cleaning the received JSON.  If validation fails, the process will retry. Increased timeouts in the vision extractor and coordinator ensure the requests don't prematurely abort. A broader search is being performed for other local API calls and timeout configurations within the project.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":false,"Summary":"Search timeouts in Pharmacy-agy-vision","UserFacing":false},"CodeContent":"import os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nroot = Path(r\"D:\\AI_Engineer\\Pharmacy-agy-vision\")\nfor py_file in root.rglob(\"*.py\"):\n    if \".git\" in py_file.parts or \".vision_cache\" in py_file.parts or \"__pycache__\" in py_file.parts:\n        continue\n    try:\n        content = py_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        lines = content.splitlines()\n        for idx, line in enumerate(lines):\n            if \"timeout\" in line.lower() and (\"requests\" in line or \"post\" in line or \"get(\" in line or \"read_timeout\" in line or \"timeout=\" in line):\n                print(f\"{py_file.relative_to(root)}:{idx+1}: {line.strip()[:100]}\")\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n","Description":"Script to search timeouts across Python files","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:/Users/kakak/.gemini/antigravity/brain/916274de-7f92-49fc-a0a8-d291664b474a/scratch/search_timeouts.py","toolAction":"Writing search script","toolSummary":"Write timeout search script"}}]}
